# Inflation: Causes, Measurement, and Control

> Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level; how it is
> measured, why it happens, and who it hurts decide both policy and
> personal finance.

[definition]
**Inflation** is a persistent increase in the general price level of
goods and services over time, which erodes the purchasing power of
money — each rupee buys less than before.
[/definition]

## 1. Measuring Inflation in India

| Feature | WPI | CPI |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Full name | Wholesale Price Index | Consumer Price Index |
| Agency | Office of the Economic Adviser, DPIIT (Commerce Ministry) | National Statistical Office (MoSPI) |
| Base year | 2011-12 | 2012 |
| Captures | Prices at the wholesale/producer level, goods only | Retail prices paid by consumers, goods *and* services |
| Policy role | Indicator of producer prices | **Inflation target (4% ±2%) set on CPI** |

Headline CPI inflation is the number the RBI's Monetary Policy
Committee answers for.

## 2. Types by Speed

| Type | Annual rate | Mood |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Creeping | Under 3% | Mild, even helpful for growth |
| Walking | 3–7% | A warning sign for policy |
| Running | 7–10% | Serious — erodes savings fast |
| Galloping | Above 10% | Prices rewrite weekly |
| **Hyperinflation** | Above roughly 50% a month | Monetary collapse |

[example]
Zimbabwe in 2008 (prices doubling in hours, the 100-trillion-dollar
note) and Weimar Germany in 1923 (wheelbarrows of banknotes for
bread) are the classic hyperinflations — both born of printing money
to finance deficits.
[/example]

## 3. Causes: Demand-Pull vs Cost-Push

- **Demand-pull** — aggregate demand outruns supply: "too much money
  chasing too few goods". Cue: war spending, credit booms, stimulus.
- **Cost-push** — rising input costs push the supply curve left:
  wages, imported oil, raw materials, taxes.

By coverage, economists also separate **headline inflation** (all
items, including volatile food and fuel) from **core inflation**
(excluding food and fuel), which shows the underlying, sticky trend
the central bank watches.

@diagram tree id="inflation-causes" layout="tree" aria="Causes of inflation"
  Inflation -> "Demand-Pull"
  Inflation -> "Cost-Push"
  "Demand-Pull" -> "Credit boom, stimulus, war spending"
  "Cost-Push" -> "Wages, oil, raw materials, taxes"
@/diagram

## 4. Who Wins and Who Loses

| Group | Effect of inflation |
| --- | --- |
| Debtors | Gain — repay loans in cheaper rupees |
| Creditors and savers | Lose — interest earned lags price rises |
| Fixed-income earners | Pensioners, salaried lose purchasing power |
| Asset holders | Real estate, equity owners often gain |
| Flexible earners | Can renegotiate and keep pace |

**Deflation** (falling prices) is not benign either: buyers postpone
purchases, revenues and jobs fall. **Stagflation** is the worst
combo — stagnation (low growth, unemployment) with persistent
inflation, classically traced to the 1970s oil shocks.

## 5. Effects in One Line Each

- Purchasing power falls; real value of savings declines.
- Lenders demand an **inflation premium** in interest rates.
- Exports get costlier, imports attractive — trade balance suffers.
- Uncertainty rises, long-term investment decisions get deferred.

## 6. Remedies

**Monetary measures (RBI):** raise the repo rate (borrowing for
banks costs more), tighten CRR (less credit multiplication), sell
government securities via open market operations (suck out money).

**Fiscal measures (Government):** cut expenditure, raise taxes,
rationalise subsidies — cooling demand directly.

[warning title="The policy dilemma"]
Fighting demand-pull inflation with tight money slows growth and
jobs; cost-push inflation is worse — tightening attacks demand but
cannot make oil cheaper. There is no painless dose, only trade-offs.
[/warning]

## 7. Quick Revision Table

| Concept | One-line memory |
| --- | --- |
| CPI vs WPI | Consumer-retail-with-services vs wholesale-goods-only |
| India's CPI base | 2012; WPI base 2011-12 |
| Target | 4% CPI inflation, band ±2% |
| Demand-pull | Too much money chasing too few goods |
| Cost-push | Rising input costs shift supply left |
| Core inflation | Excludes food and fuel |
| Hyperinflation | 50%+ a month; Zimbabwe 2008, Weimar 1923 |
| Debtors vs creditors | Debtors gain, creditors lose |

[summary]
Inflation is measured by WPI (wholesale) and CPI (retail — the
policy index, targeted at 4% ±2%), caused by excess demand or rising
costs, and it redistributes from creditors and fixed earners to
debtors and asset holders. Control is a pincer — RBI rate and
reserve tools from one side, fiscal tightening from the other.
[/summary]
